  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat is not a single future breakthrough but two compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can work autonomously, per METR's time-horizon data) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, illustrated by Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code). The author uses Terminator and Skynet as framing devices, with the line \"absolutely will not stop\" recast as \"absolutely will not stop billing,\" and contends that LLMjacking (stolen cloud credentials used to run models on a victim's bill, per Sysdig) breaks the assumption that the buyer pays, so price and access gates fail against attackers. The text also includes a probability argument that cheap parallel attempts make rare per-attempt failures add up to likely success. The tone is a persuasive, opinionated analysis aimed at engineering and security teams who consider agentic threats overhyped, with a punchy, rhetorical style, short declarative sentences, and conceptual figures illustrating the curves and billing flow.",
